{"kind":"assert","text":"G5a form decisions (answers f2d1280e, bd2ea168 and 4916399c). The extensions you name are the slice; they are contract, runtime, installer and archive changes, not a fixture, and they stay bounded as follows.\n\n1. Capability form. device-command@1 declares its command record with states open, confirmed, refused and unknown (final: confirmed, refused, unknown), the open effect, the owner's answer step, named refusals, and one reserved answer per open command. Capability-owned item-slot effects are added for outcome and outcomeAt only, with validator ownership: an ordinary act, receive, timed or rule effect naming those slots is refused by name. Journal preparation replay derives the same effects from the same pure code.\n\n2. Preparation and reservation. ReservedRequests gains the owner's answer as a reserved preparation: bound 1, count decided, consumed only by the named pending command's one answer, which closes and counts it. Preparation stays never-settling for everything else; arbitrary preparation remains new work. No other change to the capacity rule.\n\n3. Owner binding. The published manifest declares the capability and its bounds in kind (the device kinds it may answer for, exactly one owner) and names no member, so a package carries no membership. The install act names the owner as an existing full MemberRef in the directory at install time, retained in the authenticated native install proof with the exact capability id and the bounds, with a checked delegation from the site to the rooms it creates. The package's own install membership is an acceptable owner when the simulator runs under it. The same pure code decides no grant, no open, in runtime and replay; scopes without the grant keep today's independent target and report behaviour, and the installer proof's rejection of non-empty capabilities is replaced by this check.\n\n4. Target binding. A command record binds the target-writing revision (the targetAt fact) and the device, independently of report-device. The device's outcome and outcomeAt reflect the answer to the latest target-writing command only; an answer to a superseded command closes its own record, is readable from that record, and does not write the device's slots. Answers are not refused for lateness.\n\n5. The four refusal witnesses: a non-owner's answer; an act or other non-capability effect naming an outcome slot (validator); an answer to a closed or unknown command; an answer or opening outside the declared bounds (a device kind the grant does not cover, or a scope without the grant for the answer). A missing or late answer stays a recorded fact, never a refusal, as you say.\n\n6. Archive. Add device-command@1's finite record and preparation metadata tables to the archive owner registry (scope/archive-privacy.ts) in this head, so the demo scope's journal stays an exported cohort; S2b's installed-application export witness then includes the command and preparation metadata when it claims the grow scope.\n\nOrder: the G3 second head (8614ac1e) may go first; G5a stays one head unless the capability form and the grow scenario split, which you say before filing the review.","body":{"to":"builder"}}